==============================================================================
M3 POWER ANALYSIS -- required N PER GROUP
alpha = 0.05 two-sided, power = 0.80
Effect sizes are EXTERNAL (literature), never from our own simulator.
==============================================================================

     d | source / reading                   |   t-test |   ANCOVA
       |                                    |  per grp |   rho=.5
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  0.30 | small; a short, low-dose intervention |      175 |      132
  0.40 | small-to-moderate                  |       99 |       75
  0.50 | Brender et al. 2026 contrast (d = 0.50) |       63 |       48
  0.60 | optimistic                         |       44 |       33
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

THREE-ARM DESIGN (SAGE / answer-giving single agent / conventional self-study)
Total N at the reference effect size d = 0.50:
    per group (ANCOVA, rho=.5) = 48   -> total = 144

MINIMUM VIABLE variant -- the version that can still answer RQ1 only
(the attributable-share decomposition; it is a within-condition
 decomposition rather than a between-condition contrast, so it does
 NOT require detecting a group difference):
    one arm, pre-specified in `15` section 6.2
    still bounded below by the same pitfall: too few observations makes
    the criterion credit scaffolding for leak-suppression effects.

HONEST CAVEAT (must travel with every use of these numbers):
  * The normal approximation is optimistic for very small N; treat the
    small-d rows as indicative, not exact.
  * rho = 0.5 is conservative. A stronger pre-test correlation lowers N;
    a weaker one raises it above these figures.
  * Clustering matters: observations from one student are NOT independent.
    With ~20 problems per student, effective N is closer to the number of
    STUDENTS than to the number of observations. These figures are per-student.
  * Attrition and non-compliance require inflating the recruitment target
    above the analysable N (a common rule of thumb is +20%).

EXIT=0
